{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Noah.</b> HaShem informed him that with the attribute of mercy He would rescue him and his household, and from them bring life to seed for generations. This is the meaning of \"to keep seed alive upon the face of all the earth\" (Genesis 7:3), for at first He said \"to keep alive with you\" (Genesis 6:19). And now He hinted to him through the attribute of mercy concerning the offering, to inform him that He would turn to his offering, and in the merit of his offering He would sustain the world and never again cut it off because of the waters of the flood. Therefore He mentions in this verse the specific Name, for in every matter of offerings He does not mention \"Elohim,\" as I will mention (Leviticus 1:9) when I arrive there, with the help of HaShem." "<b>Come, you and all your house, into the ark.</b> Noah made the ark many days before the flood. And when the time of the flood approached, in the second month on the tenth of the month, He again commanded him to come, he and all his house, into the ark — this is what He had said to him at first (Genesis 6:18): \"you and your sons and your wife and your sons' wives.\" And He informed him that by his merit alone they would be saved, for He did not say \"I have seen you all as righteous before Me.\" And He commanded that he take and bring from the clean beast and from the clean fowl seven and seven, and He informed him of the day of the flood on which he would come into the ark. And so he did, for on that very day Noah came (Genesis 7:13). And this is the meaning of \"because of the waters of the flood\" (Genesis 7:7). Now Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 7:7): Noah too was among those of little faith, and he did not enter the ark until the waters pushed him. And the language of Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 32:6) is: he was lacking faith; had the waters not reached his ankles he would not have entered the ark. If so, it means that he did not enter there until the waters grew mighty and covered the face of all the earth, and he saw that he would drown."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the divine name used in Genesis 7:1 — YHWH (ה') — as the basis for his argument connecting the name to the attribute of mercy and to offerings."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:05.916387746Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}